A||||11||next level trends prez||questions about a turebo can anybody help||||1||
Z||000000||next level trends prez||12-29-2001||01:53 AM|||| i just bought a turbo from a friend ait wasnt for a fiero of course i have everything from the turbo it self to the intercooler to all the hoses and i have an aftermartket hks waste gate for it.. can anybody give me some insight on getting this on my car maybe fax me some diagrams over or tell me where all the little hoses go.. i know where the big ones go.. also how much horse power will i gain i have a 88 formula v6.. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||turbo time||12-29-2001||03:29 AM||||I'd say save your pennies and sell the kit. Unless you're willing to pay someone to set up the kit for you you're going to end up with a massive repair bill and a car that doesn't run. Turbo's aren't a bolt on power increase, it requires precise fuel tuning and understanding of how much the car can handle. You're not looking at a simple mod by any means. <P>Just my .02, nobody ever listens to me until it's too late, you'll probably do the same  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||12-29-2001||06:43 AM||||I agree. Although lots of people here had adapted there systems from other cars, for the most part they never work right. The only way to do it is have someone who already done it successfully, do it for you or buy a kit like IRMs that has everything, including directions. That kits around $3,000 list and its what I have on mine. Its not like bolting on a bigger carb. If you dont know what your doing, youll end up blowing up what you already have.||||reg||1
Z||000003||zeelebuba||12-30-2001||12:41 PM|||| Gee Guys thats a real Hot Rodding kinda attitude!!<P> I say go for it providing you dont use your fiero for a daily driver. Do lots a reading and dont get frustrated,and dont expect to much - It isnt a honda so dont throw 20 PSI at it.<BR>  depending what it came from there may be some varition in the lines Most are similar.<P>  On the center piece on the top, Small thd hole will be a pressure oil line. On the bottom of that is a gravity oil return<P> You may have cooling lines on the exhaust turbine if its a liquid cooled turbo.<BR> <BR> It may also have a built in wastegate if it has a diaphram on it of some sort. Dont bother with the external if thats the case<P> As far as rigging its real simple weld up some custom manifolds/exhaust<BR> <BR> The intercooler is a problem on the fiero.you could rig it up like a porsche Twin turbo on top of the moter an cool through the decklid. I built mine into the RS panel between the door and wheel tub.<P> The complex part is gonna be fuel. There are lots of options there- reprogram your ecu,modify a GM turbo ECU, Use a aftermarket ECU, Use a couple extra injectors.  <A HREF="http://www.sdsefi.com" TARGET=_blank>www.sdsefi.com</A>  has some good setups.<P> Also youe not gonna now how the turbo works till it runs- how much air it can pump,where it spools up at ETC. seeing as how turbos are expensive, moters are cheap run it till it explodes while you build another 2.8 or a 3.1. Then you can nail your rpm range down and get a proper cam/head setup going.<P> My attitude is dont be afraid of anything - Of course if uncle bob has to help you change your oil forget it- you need some mechanical skills.<P> I your comfortable with have at er' The big thing is to enjoy yourself and learn a thing or two- Dont plan on doing it on a weekend and expect to make a few mistakes along the way - guys have been reinventing the wheel for years and had a blast - Its called Hot Rodding - <P>  PS watch for my new thread later today the fiero purists are gonna flame me death- 2 12" subs in a 5 cubic foot box in a fiero LOL||||reg||1
Z||000004||turbo time||12-30-2001||02:15 PM||||a) custom manifolds for turbo's are NOT easy. They require special metals and a professional tig welding job so they don't crack. Expect to pay over $700 if you know someone to make a header for a V6<P>b) 20psi? Are you making up numbers? No way that the engine could even handle half of that without new pistons and rods.<P>c) The oil system wasn't set up for a turbo. You'll need an oil cooler system as well.<P>d) $1000 minimum to get an adequate fuel system up and running. Closer to $3000 to do it right. The stock injectors MIGHT be able to handle 5 pounds safely.<P>e) "motors are cheap, run it till it explodes"  Gee, that's what I look forward to, swapping engines.......<P>f) turbo charging isn't just a bolt on affair. You can't just take a turbo from any car and bolt it on another. If you choose the wrong turbo you can max it out at 3000 rpm or go the other way and it doesn't kick in until 4k. When you start asking for diagrams on how to hook up a turbo I highly doubt that you've done the math to see which turbo you should be doing.<P>Do some heavy research into turbo's before you even begin a project like this. <P>"My attitude is dont be afraid of anything" I agree, but don't jump into the hole without knowing how deep it is. Hot rodding is VERY expensive without knowledge. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||04:52 PM||||from experience, if it isnt a water-cooled turbo then you can just put the thing in the trash. Too much time and effort is spent trying to keep the oil from coking and seals from giving up because the turbo overheats.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000006||Turbo88||12-30-2001||06:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>from experience, if it isnt a water-cooled turbo then you can just put the thing in the trash. Too much time and effort is spent trying to keep the oil from coking and seals from giving up because the turbo overheats.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can't disagree with this more. None of the turbo cars I've built and ran have coked up. Next to no factory turbo cars use water cooled units. Water cooled units are great but for the money, do something else. Don't be afraid of building a turbo. Only racing applications use high end Inconel tubing and these are guys that run their exhausts red hot. A lower pressure lower temp application like you will find on a 2.8lt fiero will run stainless fine. Use only Mandrel bent tubing and MIG welding at the lowest. Tig if it's available. Design your system so your headers don't have to support the weight of the turbo. Fabricate a bracket and engineer a way to hang the turbo from it. <P>19lbs injectors will support 7 lbs of boost at 91 octane just fine. 19lbs injectors can be had from any tpi small block. Look for a 305cid camaro in a salvage yard. If you want to go crazy, get the 24 lbs units from a lt1. I wouldn't recommend more than 7 lbs of boost unless you are willing to spend some serious time tuning. Consider getting a MAP that can support positive manifold pressure. Think about a better fuel pump and adjustable fuel pressure regulator if you are going to push more than 5 lbs of boost. <P>Basicly, just be creative. Be willing to learn the math involved. Have fun with it! Good Luck!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||12-30-2001||06:32 PM||||Prez:  You should really invest in a good spell-checker.  Your grammar and spelling are truely horrific.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/icare.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||06:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B> I can't disagree with this more. None of the turbo cars I've built and ran have coked up. Next to no factory turbo cars use water cooled units. Water cooled units are great but for the money, do something else. Don't be afraid of building a turbo. Only racing applications use high end Inconel tubing and these are guys that run their exhausts red hot. A lower pressure lower temp application like you will find on a 2.8lt fiero will run stainless fine. Use only Mandrel bent tubing and MIG welding at the lowest. Tig if it's available. Design your system so your headers don't have to support the weight of the turbo. Fabricate a bracket and engineer a way to hang the turbo from it. <P>19lbs injectors will support 7 lbs of boost at 91 octane just fine. 19lbs injectors can be had from any tpi small block. Look for a 305cid camaro in a salvage yard. If you want to go crazy, get the 24 lbs units from a lt1. I wouldn't recommend more than 7 lbs of boost unless you are willing to spend some serious time tuning. Consider getting a MAP that can support positive manifold pressure. Think about a better fuel pump and adjustable fuel pressure regulator if you are going to push more than 5 lbs of boost. <P>Basicly, just be creative. Be willing to learn the math involved. Have fun with it! Good Luck!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>yes they can be used with no problems, but I was reffering to an individual installing and driving a turbo for thier first time, I would not recommend a non water-cooled turbo as you must learn proper shut down and time delay in order for the turbo to maintain longetivity<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000009||Turbo88||12-31-2001||09:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B> yes they can be used with no problems, but I was reffering to an individual installing and driving a turbo for thier first time, I would not recommend a non water-cooled turbo as you must learn proper shut down and time delay in order for the turbo to maintain longetivity<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thats true. I just took it for granted that someone who has done enough research to install a turbo would have learned somewhere along the way that you should let them spool down. Though one missconception I've seen is people think you still have to spool down a turbo after normal driving. I've always used this rule of thumb; "If you have been in negetive manifold pressure for 30 seconds when you pull to a stop to park, you are probably ok to shut down." That just means you should coast the car giving enough gas to get to where you want to go but it never hurts to let your car idle while you are, say, getting you laptop case out of the passenger seat, seating the emergency brake, or what ever you have to do anyway before you get out of the car. Never ever come hauling ass into a parking spot and shut down. You will coke it in a second. Turbo installations do require a little extra attention be paid to how you shut the car down but it will be rewarded with that sweet whine and good power! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||sluppy123||12-31-2001||07:01 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/good_done.jpg"> <P>Total cost was around $1k, I did all the work, SS crossover to turbo welded with SS wire in a MIG, NON water cooled turbo, mobil 1 syn oil. 4000 miles to date with no problems.  All I can say is do a ton of research before you start, and only do it If you have some damn good fab skills.<P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT- Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost,<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||SKIDMARK||12-31-2001||07:35 PM||||Sluppy, what's that turbo out of? If it works on the 2.8, will it work for a 3.4? What other engine mod's have you done to support the turbo? <BR>Looks pretty good by the way.||||reg||1
